// NEGATIVE CONTROL for `// EXPECT_CHECK_FAIL:`. The runner must report FAIL for
// this file; the self-test block inverts the verdict.
//
// This program passes `wyn check` cleanly and carries the check-phase directive
// anyway. If the runner does not report FAIL, EXPECT_CHECK_FAIL is not enforced.
//
// This control is NOT redundant with expect_fail_must_be_enforced.wyn, and the
// difference is the whole reason the stricter directive exists. EXPECT_FAIL is
// satisfied by a non-zero exit from EITHER phase, so a rule that was moved out
// of the C backend and into the checker — which is what several of this
// project's fixes actually did — still satisfies EXPECT_FAIL after it regresses,
// because the backend emits a raw clang error instead. Only a directive that
// pins the PHASE can tell a fixed compiler from a regressed one, and only this
// control can tell whether that pinning is real.
// EXPECT_CHECK_FAIL:
println("this program checks clean, so EXPECT_CHECK_FAIL must reject it")
